docs/sharing/data-exchange-policy.md:19:The current snapshot and replay formats are JSON objects with numeric `version: 1`. The board code is a string whose prefix is `np1`. Their definitions predate the general policy for new JSON exports. Every new exported JSON object has a "format" of "neon-pairs/KIND", naming what it holds, and a whole-number "version", with its keys in alphabetical order at every level. Formats that already exist stay as they are. In particular, adding a discriminator to a version 1 snapshot would make it fail the existing exact-key check, and changing the board-code string into JSON would remove its usefulness in the Board code field.
docs/competition/competition-scores.md:61:Consequently, hiding the Best statistic would not prevent saved records from changing. The display does not own the write. Similarly, suppressing one call to `localStorage.setItem` would not explain what happened to the in-memory preferences that a later effect might serialize.
docs/persistence/preferences-store.md:80:`browserStore` attempts to return `window.localStorage` and catches property-access errors, returning `null`. This catch is distinct from the catches around `getItem` and `setItem`, because the getter itself can throw before either method is available.
docs/persistence/persistence-overview.md:59:The small payload also limits synchronous work. `localStorage` uses a synchronous API, but this implementation serializes a compact aggregate instead of the whole active board repeatedly. It does not store artwork, assets or the distribution HTML there. Offline delivery comes from the built file, not from browser preference storage. [Offline delivery](../architecture/offline-delivery.md) describes that separate concern.
docs/persistence/persistence-overview.md:73:For failures, begin with the operation that failed. Accessing `window.localStorage`, reading a key, parsing its string and writing a replacement are distinct stages. [Storage failure policy](storage-failure-policy.md) covers unavailable storage and the feature behavior built around it. [Corrupt data policy](corrupt-data-policy.md) covers values that can be read but cannot be accepted. Both cases lead to usable in-memory state, but their evidence and timing differ.
docs/persistence/storage-quota-notes.md:41:The much larger disk-percentage quotas discussed for other browser storage mechanisms are not a useful capacity target for this writer. The app calls `localStorage.setItem`; it does not store its preferences in IndexedDB, Cache Storage or a file-system API. WebKit's “Updates to Storage Policy” also describes storage pressure and removal of website data, reinforcing that a browser controls lifetime as well as capacity.
docs/persistence/storage-quota-notes.md:53:| Denied storage property | Throwing `window.localStorage` getter | Matching still works when store acquisition fails |
docs/persistence/storage-failure-policy.md:25:The first operation is acquiring the storage object. `browserStore` in `src/game/session/use-saved-preferences.ts` returns `window.localStorage` inside a `try` block. If the property getter throws, the function catches the exception and returns `null`. A denied property getter is tested in `tests/browser-environment.e2e.test.ts` by defining a getter that throws a `SecurityError` before the game initializes.
docs/persistence/storage-failure-policy.md:79:The browser environment test covers a different boundary: acquisition of `window.localStorage`. It installs the throwing getter before loading the game, loads a fixed board and matches a known pair. The harness gathers uncaught page errors and checks that none occurred. That demonstrates continued play after an acquisition failure, not persistence in that denied context.
docs/persistence/corrupt-data-policy.md:93:This is different from the snapshot parser, which rejects unknown fields according to its own format. A shared JSON helper does not make all parsers equally strict. Tests that copy a snapshot rejection case into preferences without checking the reader could accidentally demand behavior the game does not implement.
docs/persistence/private-browsing-notes.md:34:When `window.localStorage` throws during acquisition, `browserStore` returns null. `useGame` captures that value for its mounted lifetime. Reading returns default preferences and later writes through the null store do nothing. The selected size and completed bests can still change inside the session, so New Game and Restart continue to work with the current in-memory preferences.
