docs/sharing/data-exchange-policy.md:3:  description: How board codes, snapshots, replays and Tournament JSON cross
docs/sharing/data-exchange-policy.md:13:The game has four exchange formats with deliberately different jobs. A board code identifies a starting deal. A snapshot describes a position on that deal. A replay describes activations on a logical timeline and produces a position when evaluated. Tournament JSON carries a bracket and, when present, the current attempt snapshot. Choosing the smallest useful format keeps a report understandable: a wrong shuffle needs a code, a wrong revealed state needs a snapshot, and a timeout ordering problem usually needs a replay.
docs/sharing/data-exchange-policy.md:19:The current snapshot and replay formats are JSON objects with numeric `version: 1`. The board code is a string whose prefix is `np1`. Their definitions predate the general policy for new JSON exports. Every new exported JSON object has a "format" of "neon-pairs/KIND", naming what it holds, and a whole-number "version", with its keys in alphabetical order at every level. Tournament JSON uses `format: "neon-pairs/tournament"` and `version: 1`; its serializer sorts every object recursively. Formats that already exist stay as they are. In particular, adding a discriminator to a version 1 snapshot would make it fail the existing exact-key check, and changing the board-code string into JSON would remove its usefulness in the Board code field.
docs/sharing/data-exchange-policy.md:33:| Can another browser inspect a finished event? | Tournament JSON | Who physically played it |
docs/sharing/data-exchange-policy.md:48:Tournament JSON carries a normalized player draft, the tournament's bracket state and an optional current game snapshot. Its exact root keys are `draft`, `format`, `gameSnapshot`, `tournament` and `version`. `Export tournament` fills the Tournament JSON box and `Load tournament` validates the whole object before applying it. A loaded completed event is added to local history unless that same event is already there; a loaded active event is saved after it is completed. The format carries results and board codes, but does not certify that those results came from an unedited game.
docs/sharing/data-exchange-policy.md:54:The reproduction panel is a native `details` element with the summary “Reproduce a game”. It contains the Board code input and Snapshot JSON textarea. Editing either field only edits a draft. `Load board` or `Load snapshot` performs the actual load. Tournament mode has a separate Tournament JSON textarea with Export tournament and Load tournament actions. This separation lets a player inspect and correct copied text without replacing a running game on each keystroke.
docs/sharing/data-exchange-policy.md:64:The pure format functions operate on strings and game data. `parseBoardCode` returns size and seed or `null`; `decodeSnapshot` returns a reconstructed state or `null`; `readReplay` returns a final state or a classified problem; `decodeTournament` returns a validated transfer or `null`. They do not operate browser controls, write storage or read files.

